KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ copy throws: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ noexcept\(int\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ throwing \(long\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The built-in __is_nothrow_constructible(T, Args...) with arguments is true
for every T (cpp_typecheck_expr.cpp: "nothrow is about exceptions, which
are not modelled"), so assertions 1 and 3 fail.  It has to construct T
from the arguments, as __is_constructible does, and check the exception
specification of the selected constructor.
